<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Blissfully offline: a lotus.

    **Third attempt, and the first two failed the same way — they read as a
    sprout.** Both drew the outer shapes as broad leaf blades sweeping outward
    and *downward* from a stem, which is exactly what foliage looks like. What
    makes a lotus a lotus is a fan of narrow petals, each pointed at the tip,
    all the same length, all radiating from one point and all tipped *upward*.

    So it is one petal drawn once and rotated five times about the base, which
    also guarantees the symmetry that was being eyeballed before. The angles stop
    at 72° because a petal laid flatter than that runs out of viewport.
-->
